            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Egypt symbolizes the unredeemed world, secular oppression, and bondage to sin from which God delivers His people.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Egypt symbolizes an apostate covenant city in bondage, specifically Jerusalem under worldly rule.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"The unredeemed world-system and bondage to sin.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A covenant city in bondage (specifically apostate Jerusalem).\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book applies the symbol exclusively to the apostate covenant city rather than the secular, unredeemed world from which believers are delivered.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus views Egypt as the external pagan world or the domain of sin; the book views it as the apostate covenant community itself.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book explicitly replaces the consensus identification of Egypt (the secular world/system of sin) with a fundamentally different referent: apostate Jerusalem. The citations provided strongly support this specific connection, particularly Revelation 11:8 which explicitly links the spiritual name 'Egypt' to the city where the Lord was crucified, and Galatians 4:25 which links present Jerusalem to bondage.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The foundational use of Egypt in the Exodus narrative portrays it as a foreign, pagan oppressor nation that holds God's people captive, not as a covenant city. Reducing the symbol to represent only apostate Jerusalem heavily depends on Revelation 11:8 while neglecting the much more pervasive Exodus typology found throughout the rest of Scripture.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
